## The oracle

RUBY'S OWN CHECKBOX INPUT VIEW. The checkbox widget is Ruby's
card-mod-list checkbox editor: the view is registered beside the other
input views (card-mod-list-0.20.0/set/abstract/01_list/html_views.rb:56-58)
and rendered by `checkbox_input` (:64-65), whose template is
card-mod-list-0.20.0/set/abstract/01_list/html_views/checkbox_input.haml:1-8:
the wrapper class is `pointer-checkbox-list` (:1), each option's element id
is `pointer-checkbox-#{option_name.to_name.key}` (:3), the checked state
comes from the card's own item names (:4), and the input is
`check_box_tag "pointer_checkbox-#{unique_id}", option_name, checked,
id: id, class: "pointer-checkbox-button ..."` (:6-7).

RUBY'S OWN RULE SAYS CHECKBOX IS AN OFFERED INPUT TYPE. The input type
right answers `supports_content_option_view?` for checkbox and radio
(card-mod-edit-0.20.0/set/right/input_type.rb:39-41), and the list type
offers the checkbox input among its content options
(card-mod-edit-0.20.0/set/type/list.rb:1-3).

RUBY'S OWN FEATURE IS THE BEHAVIOURAL SPECIFICATION. Ruby's own
pointer-inputs feature carries the checkbox scenario
(decko-0.20.0/features/pointer_inputs.feature:27-38): create the
`friends+*right+*input type` rule with the content "checkbox" (:28), go to
the pointer card (:29), check a value (:30), submit (:31), and the card
shows the checked value (:32-33); then edit it (:34), uncheck it (:35),
save and close (:36), and the card no longer shows it (:37-38). The
harness's check is that scenario, driven through the port.

THE PORT RENDERS THE SAME WIDGET. src/card/mod-loader/mods/card-mod-list.ts:626-646
is the port's checkboxInputHtml: the wrapper is `<div
class='pointer-checkbox-list'>`, each option's id is
`pointer-checkbox-${key}` where key is the option name's key, the input is
`<input type="checkbox" name="pointer_checkbox-${uniqueId}" id="${id}"
value="${option}" class="pointer-checkbox-button ">` and the checked
attribute rides the card's own item names, with the option's label beside
it. The input type is dispatched from the card's `*input_type` rule
(card-mod-list.ts:467-478, the checkbox arm at :474).

## What is oracle-matched and what is not, stated plainly

Oracle-matched: the widget's identity is the one Ruby's checkbox editor
renders (the `pointer-checkbox-list` wrapper, the
`pointer-checkbox-<key>` element id, the `pointer_checkbox-<unique id>`
input name, the `pointer-checkbox-button` class, the checked state from
the card's item names: Ruby checkbox_input.haml:1-8, the port
card-mod-list.ts:626-646); the behaviour is the one Ruby's own feature
specifies, including the uncheck arm (pointer_inputs.feature:27-38); and
the choice is STORED and shown on the card's own page, which the check
reads from the database and the rendered page (:376-380).

NOT oracle-matched, and stated rather than glossed over: the fixture the
check builds (the option type, the option cards, the `*right+
